for all the people out there shouting  that Edward Snowden should have gone  through the the proper channels Tom  Drake and Bill Binney Kirk Wiebe and Ed  Loomis did go through the proper  channels and all of them fell under  criminal investigation for having done  so I am Nick Gillespie with Reason TV  and today we're talking with jesselyn  Radack she's a lawyer who is represented  many whistleblowers such as John  Kiriakou Thomas Drake and Edward Snowden  she's a whistleblower herself as well  and she's now the national security and  Human Rights director had exposed facts  jessalyn thanks for talking to us thank  you for having me let's work through  some of your kind of greatest hits and  then we'll talk about larger issues  related to surveillance and the security  state you used to work at the Department  of Justice during and during the  prosecution of John Walker Lindh the  American Taliban from what a dozen years  ago almost now what happened in that  case and how did that change your entire  framework of thinking about government  accountability I was in the ethics  attorney for the Justice Department you  know despite giving advice not to  interrogate the so-called American  Taliban without his lawyer and also not  to torture him it was clear the US was  doing both and then I advised not to use  that information in a criminal  prosecution I was informed unambiguously  that he had counsel and because of that  like with any American I would say if he  has counsel you need to allow him his  attorney for any kind of custodial  interrogation that was on a Friday and  the Justice Department office said I was  dealing with called back on Monday and  said well the FBI interrogated him  anyway what do we do now I said well you  can use that information for national  security and intelligence gathering but  not for criminal prosecution because it  was an improper interrogation without  kids and when the criminal prosecution  came up all of your emails on this went  missing from the official record that's  correct  there was a department-wide discovery  you order for all Justice Department  correspondent stealing with John Walker  Lindh interrogation the prosecutor had  two of mine but I had written more than  a dozen that were reflected pretty badly  on the FBI and I went to check the file  back then we kept hard copies of things  and they were not in the file I  resurrected them from my computer  archives and wrote a memo to my boss and  attached them and resigned as I'm not  going to be a part of whatever is going  on what had brought you to the  Department of Justice that was my career  ambition was to be a lifelong public  servant but then when it got completely  politicized in my case after 9/11 to the  point that we were looking the other way  when there are pictures trophy photos  going around the world of an American  being tortured by our people it really  caused a crisis of conscience for me  let's talk about torture for a second  because one of the people you did  represent was John Kiriakou who brought  torture to the forefront what did he do  and what happened to him John Kiriakou  was the first CIA agent who actually  publicly confirmed that torture was an  official United States program that we  were waterboarding people and said  unequivocally torture is wrong and we  shouldn't be in the business of doing it  during the subsequent years after that  interviewed the CIA filed six crimes a  report spawned carry a coup trying to  get the Justice Department to prosecute  him and it wasn't until the Obama  administration's opened war on  whistleblowers using the Espionage Act  that he wasn't that prosecuted on a  couple of counts of espionage and to  violating the intelligence identity  protection act like a number of  Espionage Act defendants he didn't  actually plead guilty to committing  espionage what did he end up  sentence John ended up getting 30 months  in jail and served two and a half years  in jail and part of that under house  arrest and now he's on probation but  really this is one of the smartest  people I've ever met  speaks multiple languages including ones  we need right now for people in  government service like Arabic and he  will never work in the government again  though he remains staunchly patriotic I  mean most of the people that I represent  have spent their entire adult lives  serving in the military or working for  the government they are true believers  and I think they still do believe that  this can be fixed or they wouldn't have  blown the whistle and they wouldn't  continue to speak out they feel like we  had we're not at the point of no return  that there still can be some sort of  pull back on this well that describes in  many ways Thomas Drake one of your  clients who along with people like  William Binney and Kirk Wiebe went  through the official chat where they  went through what channels seemed to be  official at the time Drake was an NSA  whistleblower who talked about wire  warrantless wiretapping what happened to  him Thomas Drake went through every  conceivable internal channel about a  number of surveillance programs he was  talking mostly it one called trailblazer  but he went to his boss he went to the  NSA general counsel he went to the House  and Senate intelligence committees and  testified in to 911 inquiries and not  only did they fail to redress his  concern they turned around and  prosecuted him and prosecuted him for  espionage so for all the people out  there shouting that Edward Snowden  should have gone through the proper  channels first of all they're not that  many channels for national security and  intelligence whistleblowers they are  excluded from most avenues available to  other was  but second of all Tom Drake and Bill  Binney Kirk Wiebe and Ed Loomis did go  through the proper channels and all of  them fell under criminal investigation  for having done so let's talk about  Snowden your most famous client who  recently joined Twitter and immediat  almost immediately got over a million  followers what is different about  Snowden the appeal that he seems to have  to a wide range of people who otherwise  look askance at whistleblowers I think  with Snowden there a number of  differences first of all he has publicly  said that he studied the cases of Tom  Drake and Bill Binney and how they had  gone through proper channels and how  they got completely lambasted and  skewered for doing that  and made his decision differently to go  directly to reporters give the  information to reporters and let them  use their editorial judgment about what  was in the public interest to know he  also had what a lot of whistleblowers  don't have which is documentary evidence  that's very hard to obtain or actually  remove from an agency he's young he  appeals to many different communities he  appeals to a very youthful activist  community he appeals to civil  libertarians he appeals to progressive  Democrats he has broad cross-section  appeal he's incredibly articulate and he  doesn't want to be the center of  attention now that's a problem for every  was the blower they want to shoot the  messenger rather than listen to the  message what is the likely disposition  of his case over the coming 10 years or  so will he ever be able to come back to  the United States in anything other than  chains I think he will eventually be  able to come back to the United States I  don't think it will be within the next  ten years necessarily though since 2013  we have seen a big shift in US support  of him  40% of people you know 60% thought it  was terrible 40% thought he was had done  a good thing and now those numbers are  reversed a lot he has a lot more public  support but like the public support he  enjoys and countries around the world  the political leadership is still not  going to treat him well and in fact he  just said and the BBC interview he did  that the Department of Justice has not  offered him 